{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CfSaygrkR93j4EHB5UbyG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my analysis of the repository, here's what I found:\n\n**Requirement, restated:** Add a `POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel` endpoint that transitions an order's status from `ACTIVE` to `CANCELLED`, cancels all `CREATED` shipments, and provides proper error handling.\n\n**Repository findings:**\n- Order entity (Order.kt:11-16) has no status field currently\n- OrderController (OrderController.kt:15-46) provides create, getById, and list endpoints\n- OrderRepository (OrderRepository.kt:10-27) is an in-memory implementation\n- Shipment entity (Shipment.kt:9-14) already has a status field with CREATED, CONFIRMED, CANCELLED values\n- ShipmentRepository (ShipmentRepository.kt:10-27) lacks a method to find shipments by orderId\n- ApiExceptions (ApiExceptions.kt:11-30) define ResourceNotFoundException (404) and ConflictException (409)\n- ApiError.kt (31-37) defines the ErrorCode enum\n- Tests use MockMvc to verify both status codes and error body structure\n\n**Conventions this repository already has:**\n- Sealed class ApiException hierarchy for different error types\n- GlobalExceptionHandler for centralized error response formatting\n- Machine-readable ErrorCode enum in all error responses\n- In-memory repositories for testing without external infrastructure\n- Spring Boot + Kotlin with exhaustive when patterns\n\n**Affected files:**\n1. `Order.kt` - add OrderStatus enum and status field\n2. `OrderController.kt` - add POST cancel endpoint\n3. `ShipmentRepository.kt` - add method to find shipments by orderId\n4. `ShipmentController.kt` - validate cancelled orders cannot receive new shipments\n5. `ApiError.kt` - add ORDER_CANCELLED error code\n6. `OrderControllerTest.kt` - add comprehensive cancel tests\n\n**Risks:**\n- Order object structure change could break serialization\n- Need atomic transaction-like behavior (all-or-nothing for cancel)\n- Idempotent retry behavior must work for already-cancelled orders\n- Confirmed shipments must block cancel without side effects\n\n**Open questions:** Addressed by the requirement - error code for cancelled order rejection is 409 (ConflictException).\n\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n\n**Proposed change, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt**: Add an `OrderStatus` enum with `ACTIVE` and `CANCELLED` values. Add a `status: OrderStatus` field to the Order data class, defaulting to `ACTIVE` for new orders.\n\n2. **ApiError.kt**: Add `ORDER_CANCELLED` to the ErrorCode enum to represent when a shipment cannot be created for a cancelled order.\n\n3. **OrderController.kt**: Add a `cancel` method annotated with `@PostMapping(\"/{orderId}/cancel\")` that:\n   - Finds the order or throws ResourceNotFoundException\n   - If already CANCELLED, return it (idempotent)\n   - If any shipment is CONFIRMED, throw ConflictException without modifying anything\n   - Otherwise, set order status to CANCELLED, save it, and set all CREATED shipments to CANCELLED\n   - Return the updated order\n\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt**: Add `findByOrderId(orderId: String)` method to retrieve all shipments for an order.\n\n5. **ShipmentController.kt**: In the `create` method, after checking for duplicate shipmentId, check if the order exists and is not CANCELLED. If CANCELLED, throw ConflictException with ORDER_CANCELLED code.\n\n6. **OrderControllerTest.kt**: Add tests for:\n   - Cancel an active order with created shipments\n   - Idempotent cancel (cancel already-cancelled order)\n   - Cancel blocked by confirmed shipment\n   - Cancel non-existent order returns 404\n   - Cancel returns the updated order with CANCELLED status\n\n**Alternative rejected:**\n- Could use a ServiceLayer/OrderService class for business logic, but existing controllers handle logic directly with repositories, so I'll follow the same pattern for consistency.\n- Could add new exceptions for order-specific errors, but ConflictException is already used for incompatible states (SHIPMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS uses it), so ORDER_CANCELLED fits the pattern.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n1. **Cancel active order with CREATED shipments:**\n   - Request: POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel\n   - Controller finds order by id → throws ResourceNotFoundException if not found (404)\n   - Check if order.status == CANCELLED → return it immediately (200, idempotent)\n   - Find all shipments for orderId\n   - Check if any shipment.status == CONFIRMED → throw ConflictException(ORDER_CONFIRMED_CONFLICT or similar) without modifying (409)\n   - Set order.status = CANCELLED, save order\n   - Set all CREATED shipments to CANCELLED, save each\n   - Return: 200 with updated order\n\n2. **Cancel already-cancelled order:**\n   - Same as above but returns immediately after status check (200)\n\n3. **Cancel blocked by confirmed shipment:**\n   - Check fails, throw ConflictException before modifying anything\n   - Response: 409 with error code (need to define)\n\n4. **Cancel non-existent order:**\n   - Order not found\n   - Response: 404 ORDER_NOT_FOUND\n\n5. **Create shipment for cancelled order:**\n   - ShipmentController checks order status before creating\n   - Response: 409 with ORDER_CANCELLED error code\n\n**Test strategy:**\n- Test 1: Cancel ACTIVE order with CREATED shipments → verify order becomes CANCELLED, shipments become CANCELLED\n- Test 2: Cancel already-CANCELLED order → verify no error, returns CANCELLED order (idempotent)\n- Test 3: Try to cancel order with CONFIRMED shipment → verify 409, order stays ACTIVE, shipments unchanged\n- Test 4: Try to cancel non-existent order → verify 404\n- Test 5: Try to create shipment for CANCELLED order → verify 409 ORDER_CANCELLED\n- Test 6: Verify cancelled order is returned with CANCELLED status in GET\n\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n\nNow I'll implement these changes:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usa